From c41256cb7a01650a27743ec526756db253ed3730 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Wolfe <rnwolfe@google.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 10:15:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] bgpd: resolve EVPN gateway IPs against local and symmetric
 IRB hosts

With "enable-resolve-overlay-index", a type-5 route that carries a gateway
IP overlay index (RFC 9136) is only used once its gateway is resolved: NHT
must reach the gateway over the SVI of an L2VNI, and the gateway must be
in that EVI's remote IP hash, which holds the IPs of the imported MAC/IP
routes. Two common placements of the gateway never resolve, and the path
stays invalid with "overlay index unresolved":

- The gateway is attached to the receiving VTEP. Its MAC/IP route is
  locally originated and never enters the remote IP hash. This is the
  VTEP that has to use the gateway IP: without it, the route points at
  the exporting VTEP, which reaches the gateway back through this one.
- The gateway is attached to another VTEP in a symmetric IRB fabric. The
  host route installed from its MAC/IP route is more specific than the
  subnet, so NHT resolves the gateway over the L3VNI SVI, which is not an
  L2VNI SVI.

bgp_evpn_is_gateway_ip_resolved() now takes the gateway as known in an
EVI when the EVI has a remote MAC/IP route for it (the remote IP hash, as
before) or a locally originated one (a lookup in the EVI's IP table, which
is keyed by IP). When NHT resolved the gateway over the tenant VRF's L3VNI
SVI, it looks in every L2VNI of that VRF.

The state has to follow MAC/IP routes as they come and go.
bgp_evpn_gateway_ip_reevaluate() recomputes it with
bgp_evpn_is_gateway_ip_resolved() and evaluates the paths when it changes.
It runs when a remote MAC/IP route enters or leaves the remote IP hash, in
place of the forced resolve or unresolve, and when a local MAC/IP route is
added or deleted (update_evpn_route(), delete_evpn_route(),
delete_vni_type2_routes()). Recomputing keeps a gateway resolved while
another route still knows it, for example when a host moves from a remote
VTEP to this one and the remote route is withdrawn after the local one is
added. bgp_evpn_remote_ip_process_nexthops() is now only used when the
EVI's SVI or VRF changes or the EVI goes away, and also acts on gateways
resolved over the L3VNI SVI. Such a gateway stays resolved while another
EVI of the VRF still knows it, which is the rule
bgp_evpn_is_gateway_ip_resolved() applies.

"enable-resolve-overlay-index" also gets configured while the routes
already exist, which is how it reaches running switches. Its set handler
only filled the remote IP hash, so a gateway known from a local MAC/IP
route stayed unresolved until that route changed. The set handler now
re-evaluates the gateway for every MAC/IP route of the EVI, and the unset
handler unresolves the gateways known from local MAC/IP routes too. It
passes a new force argument so that every gateway is unresolved, also one
that another EVI still knows.

delete_all_vni_routes() unlinked paths from the remote IP hash in its MAC
table loop, where the MAC-only routes are never in the hash, so a VNI
going down left freed paths in it. Unlink them in the IP table loop.

The installed route does not change: the path still goes to zebra with
the gateway as a recursive nexthop, so traffic follows the gateway's own
route, the L2VNI SVI for a local host and the host route for a remote one.

On 10.5 this needs the previous commit (FRRouting/frr ae614f4871):
without it evaluate_paths() does not run bestpath when only the gateway's
MAC/IP state changes, so the path turns valid but is never selected.

tests/topotests/bgp_evpn_overlay_index_gateway_irb checks that receivers
over eBGP and iBGP install a type-5 route through its gateway when the
gateway is a local host and when it is a remote host behind a symmetric
IRB host route, for IPv4 and IPv6, also when
"enable-resolve-overlay-index" is configured after the routes exist, that
the route follows the host being forgotten and learnt again, and that it
stays when a second L2VNI that also knows the gateway leaves the VRF.
Those checks fail without this change. bgp_evpn_rt5, bgp_evpn_rt5_addpath,
bgp_evpn_vxlan_topo1, bgp_evpn_vxlan_svd_topo1,
bgp_evpn_vxlan_macvrf_soo_topo1 and bgp_evpn_mh pass.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Wolfe <rnwolfe@google.com>
